// core/services - the ergonomic Frust half of plugin-to-plugin service
// discovery (docs/17-Plugin-Automation-Layer.md section 4.3). The
// native registry, ownership tracking, and per-plugin cleanup-on-unload
// live in frust_plugin_host (C++) - this file exists so a plugin author
// never writes those extern fn declarations themselves.
//
// This is the third connection frust_plugin_host provides, distinct
// from the other two: core/events.fr is host-fires/plugin-listens;
// frust_plugin_register_host_function is host-gives-plugin-something-
// to-call. This one is plugin-to-plugin - one loaded plugin registers a
// capability under a name, and ANY other loaded plugin (or the host)
// can look it up by that name and use it, without either side needing
// to already know which specific plugin provides it.
//
// Service values are deliberately opaque (String, the same "just an
// opaque ptr" convention used everywhere else in this codebase) -
// domain-agnostic by requirement, so this layer cannot know or
// constrain what a given service actually is. That meaning is defined
// entirely by agreement between whoever registers a specific service
// name and whoever looks it up.
//
// IMPORTANT: register_service must be called from within a plugin's own
// on_init() - that's how frust_plugin_host knows which plugin owns the
// registration, so it can automatically remove it when that plugin is
// unloaded. Calling it from anywhere else still works, but the
// registration won't be cleaned up automatically.

extern fn frust_register_service(name: String, service: String);
extern fn frust_lookup_service(name: String) -> String;

pub fn register_service(name: String, service: String) = {
    frust_register_service(name, service);
}

pub fn lookup_service(name: String) -> String = {
    frust_lookup_service(name)
}
